WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.140
"How Generosity May Help You
Be Happier and Live Longer"

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:12.120
Many things make people happy,
from eating foods to cashing checks.

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:14.880
But alas, the more we experience
some dose of pleasure,

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:16.800
the less pleasurable the dose.

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:19.560
This is what’s called
hedonic adaptation,

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:24.160
how the first bite of chocolate
always tastes better than the last.

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:29.800
How can we sustainably increase the
well-being of the general population?

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:34.520
What about “being good
by doing good”?

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:37.480
Beginning in the late 1980s,

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:41.000
reports began surfacing
of the “helper’s high”—

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:44.400
feelings of elation, exhilaration,
and increased energy,

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:49.080
following selfless service to others,
and then a period of calm and serenity.

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:54.240
The “high” was thought to be
like a post-intense-workout rush.

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:56.680
Some are cynical about
the helper’s high,

00:00:56.680 --> 00:00:59.720
arguing that helpers
engage in faux generosity

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:03.520
because of a selfish desire to
feel good about themselves.

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:05.200
As this author put it,

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:09.520
“perhaps it’s no accident that these
naysayers often seem selfish,

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:12.240
grouchy, grumpy, testy, irritable…”.

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:15.560
They deny themselves the
positive emotional benefits

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:17.080
that flow from helping others,

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:20.240
and therefore miss out on
the happiness and exhilaration

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:23.960
that are part of the helper’s high.

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:27.200
Prosocial behavior, engaging
in actions that benefit others,

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:29.920
is considered a bedrock of society.

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:33.000
Whether communities thrive hinges
on the willingness of their members

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:36.600
to cooperate with one another
and help those in need.

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:39.880
The central role of prosocial
behavior is supported by research

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:43.600
showing that infants as young
as three months of age

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:47.440
pay attention to the prosocial and
antisocial actions of others

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:50.560
and strongly prefer prosocial people.

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:53.880
There is also evidence that giving
leads to happiness in young children.

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:59.360
By age two, toddlers are happier
giving away treats to others

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:02.440
than receiving treats themselves.

00:02:02.440 --> 00:02:05.360
And if it’s their own treat
they’re giving away,

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:07.240
they become even happier!

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:08.640
And, as we mature,

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:11.360
spending money on others
appears to promote happiness,

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:15.880
whereas spending on
ourselves may not.

00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:18.720
For these cooperative
behaviors to have continued

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:20.960
throughout our evolution
as a species,

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:23.320
there must be biological
mechanisms

00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:25.320
that sustain these
prosocial actions,

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:28.800
despite their inherent
costs to us as individuals.

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:31.520
When we display empathy
towards others,

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:34.800
we get a boost in the so-called
“love hormone” oxytocin.

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:37.960
Then the high we get from that
can get us to give more,

00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:39.760
in a virtuous cycle.

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:43.711
In one study, people were infused
with oxytocin or placebo

00:02:43.711 --> 00:02:45.480
and were then asked to
make a one-shot decision

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:48.080
on how to split some
money with a stranger.

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:52.560
Those who had gotten the
oxytocin were 80% more generous.

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:56.800
Kindness may even affect
us on an epigenetic level.

00:02:56.800 --> 00:02:59.640
Could a 4-week kindness
intervention reduce the expression

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:02.280
of a stress-related
gene signature?

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:06.360
Known as the Conserved Transcriptional
Response to Adversity (CTRA),

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:09.960
it is characterized by increased
expressions of pro-inflammatory genes,

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:15.200
and the immunosuppression we get from
a flight-or-flight type of stress response.

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:19.480
And indeed, those randomized to
perform acts of kindness for others,

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:20.760
rather than themselves,

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:26.240
significantly reduced the
expression of these stress genes.

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:31.320
So, could simple interventions lead to
sustainable increases in our well-being?

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:33.360
Apparently so.

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:35.480
Something as small as
remembering a time

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:37.280
you bought something
for someone else

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:40.880
can make you feel significantly
happier right after this recollection,

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:43.760
and the happiness runs
in a circular loop.

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:46.520
Prosocial spending
increases happiness,

00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:49.560
which in turn encourages
prosocial spending.

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:52.360
And, rather than getting
tired of the same old thing,

00:03:52.360 --> 00:03:54.960
in this case,
being kind to others,

00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:59.800
people seem to be slow to
adapt to the warm glow of giving.

00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:00.480
For five days,

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:03.760
about a hundred individuals either
got money to spend on themselves

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:05.680
or give away to someone else.

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:08.240
Those who got the
money were all happy,

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:09.440
but receiving the same amount

00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:13.280
to spend on themselves every
day kinda got old over time.

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:15.360
They started adapting to it.

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:17.320
But those giving away
the same amount

00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:20.040
experienced consistently
higher happiness levels

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:23.000
that did not significantly
fade over time.

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:26.280
The benefits of giving to
others appear universal

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:28.440
across age, gender,
and income,

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:32.720
and even the skeptics who didn’t
think they’d be happier by giving,

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:35.640
in fact ended up happier
when they did.

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:38.400
And cross-culturally, too.

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:43.080
The well-being that comes from giving
may be a “psychological universal.”

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:45.840
Based on survey data
from 136 countries,

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:49.640
giving is associated with greater
happiness around the world,

00:04:49.640 --> 00:04:52.680
suggesting that the reward
experienced from helping others

00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:56.520
may be deeply ingrained
in our human nature.

00:04:56.520 --> 00:04:59.760
And the benefits may even
extend beyond happiness.

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:03.080
Conventional wisdom suggests
that self-care or “treating oneself”

00:05:03.080 --> 00:05:06.240
is the best way to reduce stress
and promote physical health,

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:10.160
but evidence seems to show that
outwardly focusing our efforts—

00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:12.840
prosocial behavior rather
than being self-focused—

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:15.080
may make us healthiest.

00:05:15.080 --> 00:05:18.000
For example, older adults
who gave the gift of their time

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:20.920
by volunteering at two
or more organizations

00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:23.840
had up to five times
lower mortality rates

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:26.280
than those who volunteered
minimally or not at all.

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:28.360
That’s a massive difference.

00:05:28.360 --> 00:05:31.000
And the significantly lower
risk of premature death

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:33.960
was found even after controlling
for confounding factors

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:35.320
like physical functioning,

00:05:35.320 --> 00:05:38.520
religious affiliation,
or social support.

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:41.920
So, instead of just hoping
we stumble onto happiness,

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:44.720
why not run right towards
it with open arms—

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:49.702
and do some good for
the world, and ourselves.

